##############################################################################
# WARRaNT Example: LL1 Containership — Area-Wide GNSS Spoofing and Navigation
#                  Situational Awareness Degradation
# File: examples/example-gnss-spoofing.ttl
# Instance namespace: https://warrant-project.eu/data/ll1#
# Ontology version: 0.10-poc
# Source documents:
#   - Block diagram of bridge navigation equipment (H/N 4009/4010/4011/4012)
#   - Bridge navigation equipment interconnection interface details
#   - Cyber security Equipment sw list V145-148 (vessel CATHERINE C, V145)
#   - FMEA OT V145-148 (Navigation/Communication OT + Electrical/Automation OT)
#   - WARRaNT LL1 scenario note (AIS/ECDIS spoofing), reworked here into the
#     primary GNSS scenario and a separate direct AIS spoofing scenario
#
# Scope: Danaos containership fleet (hull 4009–4012). Navigation & Comms OT.
#
# Vessel functions modelled:
#   VF-NAV-001  Navigation (position fixing, chart display, course keeping)
#   VF-COM-001  Communication (VHF/MF-HF/Inmarsat, distress alerting)
#   VF-VDR-001  Voyage Data Recording (black-box, accident investigation)
#   VF-EL-001   Electrical OT & Alarm Monitoring (AMS, fire detection, BNWAS)
#
# ── PRIMARY USE CASE ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Area-wide GNSS spoofing degrades the navigation situational awareness of the
# bridge. On a coastal approach in dense traffic, a counterfeit GNSS signal
# covers the sea area. Every receiver in range, ours and those of the vessels
# around us, computes a position that is plausible but wrong. The equipment
# stays available throughout; the information it carries is no longer
# trustworthy. WARRaNT evaluates the dependability of the navigation function,
# not the availability of the equipment.
#
#   external GNSS spoofing (radio, not network)
#   → GPS No1 and No2 output a plausible but wrong fix           DevGPSSpoofed
#   → A. ECDIS correctly displays own ship at the wrong position
#        → officer's own-ship position understanding is wrong   WrongPositionProcessModelFlaw
#        → wrong course alteration                              WrongCourseAlterationUCA
#   → B. our AIS correctly broadcasts the wrong own-ship position
#        → others see us in the wrong place                     DevOwnShipAISBroadcastWrong
#   → C. nearby vessels' GNSS is spoofed too; their AIS correctly
#        broadcasts wrong positions; our AIS receives valid messages;
#        ECDIS shows the traffic in the wrong place              DevSurroundingTrafficAISPositionsWrong
#        → officer's traffic understanding is wrong             WrongTrafficPictureProcessModelFlaw
#        → wrong collision-avoidance manoeuvre                  WrongCollisionAvoidanceUCA
#   → standing into danger, close-quarters situation, collision or grounding
#                                                               HazHazardousAreaApproach
#
# Neither B nor C is AIS spoofing: the AIS equipment and messages are genuine
# and carry positions that were wrong at their GNSS source. ECDIS is the
# consumer and presentation layer, not the source of the error.
#
# Detection rests on cross-source inconsistency against independent evidence:
# radar ranges and bearings to charted features, echo-sounder depth against
# charted depth, dead reckoning from gyro and speed log, and the pattern of
# AIS-to-radar divergence across many targets. Agreement between GNSS-derived
# sources proves nothing: GPS No1 and No2 see the same counterfeit signal, our
# AIS repeats our GNSS, and nearby vessels' AIS repeats theirs. Radar-derived
# latitude and longitude are geo-referenced with our own GNSS fix and are not
# independent either; radar range and bearing are. Carrier-to-noise density
# does not identify spoofing.
#
# Secondary scenario, kept separate: direct AIS spoofing (counterfeit AIS
# messages, phantom or displaced targets). It falsifies the traffic picture
# only; own-ship GNSS position stays correct. ECDIS compromise, VDR tampering,
# AMS corruption and the other FMEA entries are design-time knowledge in the
# KG with their own detection events, dated outside the scenario, and take no
# part in the GNSS causal chain.
#
# Runtime timeline (2026-06-01, coastal approach, manual operation):
#   08:10  last logged independent position fix
#   ~09:30 spoofing begins; GNSS fix drifts away from the true track
#   09:44:30 GNSS vs radar-fix / depth / DR residual detected
#   09:45:00 coherent AIS-to-radar divergence across surrounding targets
#   09:45:05 attributes, indices, triggers; DI_sys forecast below floor
#   09:46:30 REDS ranks three responses
#   09:48–09:55 officer authorises and completes reversion to independent
#          position fixing and radar-based collision avoidance
#
# ── PROVENANCE OF THE CONTENT ──────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# DERIVED. The equipment inventory, the interconnections and interface types,
# the failure modes with their FMEA severity, occurrence, detection and RPN
# figures, and the functional breakdown come from the Danaos source documents
# listed above. The security zones, asset roles, threat scenarios,
# vulnerabilities and controls are a reading of those documents against
# IEC 62443-3-2 and IACS UR E26. The Danaos FMEA covers GNSS jamming but has
# no GNSS spoofing entry, so the spoofing deviation carries no FMEA ratings.
#
# WARRaNT LL1 BASELINE. The scenario and everything in the runtime supervision
# layer and the Living Dependability Case that carries a number (attribute
# values, mode weights, state thresholds, damping factor, aggregation weights,
# management floor, trend window, REDS weights, Assurance Level weights and the
# readiness mapping), together with the claim wording and the evidence
# obligations, are WARRaNT-defined rather than taken from Danaos documents.
# They form the approved LL1 demonstration baseline: every such artefact
# carries warrant:hasApprovalStatus "APPROVED" with
# warrant:approvedBy "WARRaNT LL1 use-case team". Every index is derivable by
# hand; the derivations are given at the head of the runtime section.
#
# ── MODELLING ASSUMPTIONS ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Technical and modelling
#   A1  The spoofer is a single transmitter, so affected receivers converge on
#       a common false position; this is what makes the coherent AIS-to-radar
#       divergence detectable. A spoofer imposing a different offset on each
#       vessel would weaken that signature.
#   A2  The GP-170 receivers are treated as GPS-only; no detection relies on
#       receiver autonomous integrity monitoring or constellation disagreement.
#   A3  The autopilot is in track-control mode when the spoofing begins. That it
#       receives GPS is from the equipment inventory; the mode is assumed.
#   A4  Radar echo range and bearing are independent of GNSS; radar-derived
#       latitude and longitude, the chart overlay and ground-stabilised vectors
#       are not.
#   A5  Charted radar-conspicuous features are within radar range throughout the
#       coastal approach (asserted as ll1:ASM_IndependentFixFeaturesInRange).
#   A6  Surrounding vessels are modelled as data flows, not as vessel models.
#   A7  The ontology has no integrity attribute. Availability is assessed from
#       equipment and data-feed delivery; information integrity is carried by
#       the Cybersecurity attribute, with Reliability (correct position
#       solution) and Safety (consequence).
#   A8  GNSS spoofing propagates as hazard class CYBER_THREAT, not DATA_LOSS.
#   A9  The position-data index takes part in risk propagation only and is not
#       aggregated into the system index, which would count it twice.
#   A10 The wrong own-ship AIS broadcast is inferred from the own-ship GNSS
#       detection through ll1:Dep_PositionData_to_AIS; there is no external
#       confirmation such as a VTS report.
#   A11 The FMEA-baseline and direct AIS spoofing detection events are dated on
#       earlier legs (May 2026) as illustrations, not a record of real
#       incidents; the VDR and AMS findings from those dates are closed.
#   A12 The communication-to-navigation IT/OT propagation edge is kept as a
#       structural background term (0.03 of R_nav); it is not part of the GNSS
#       attack chain.
#   A13 Scenario figures are illustrative: the 1.2 NM offset, 38 m against
#       12 m depth, 11 of 14 targets converging within 0.4 NM, the timeline.
#
# Numbers and governance
#   A14 Approval authority for the WARRaNT-defined baseline is the WARRaNT LL1
#       use-case team; the FMEA-derived controls keep their Danaos approval.
#   A15 Attribute weights, threshold sets, management floor 0.55, trend window
#       6 h, damping factor 0.50, REDS weights 0.3/0.2 and Assurance Level
#       weights are carried over. New values: position-data weights 0.6/0.4 and
#       beta 0.20; local risks 0.60 (position data), 0.09 and 0.04 (navigation
#       before and after the response); other functions at a 0.80 baseline;
#       the four Digital Twin forecasts.
#   A16 A REDS alternative is admissible only if its expected DI_sys is at or
#       above the management floor.
#   A17 Readiness = (AL + RC)/2 − 0.5·NSI, with RC the share of requirements
#       whose expected obligations are all PRESENT and NSI the unresolved
#       nonconformity severities (MAJOR 0.8, MINOR 0.4) over the number of
#       requirements.
#   A18 The company navigation procedure requires an independent position fix
#       at least hourly in coastal waters and immediately whenever GNSS
#       integrity is in doubt.
#   A19 The trend slope is the endpoint difference over the trend window, so a
#       sudden integrity loss is not a sustained decline.
#   A20 ll1:ASM_BridgeAccessControlled stays UNDER_REVIEW: a runtime assurance
#       state following the May ECDIS configuration event, not a pending
#       approval.
#
##############################################################################

@prefix ll1:     <https://warrant-project.eu/data/ll1#> .
@prefix warrant: <https://warrant-project.eu/ontology/core#> .
@prefix davom:   <https://warrant-project.eu/ontology/davom#> .
@prefix obs:     <https://warrant-project.eu/ontology/observation#> .
@prefix cdm:     <https://warrant-project.eu/ontology/cdm#> .
@prefix assr:    <https://warrant-project.eu/ontology/assurance#> .
@prefix di:      <https://warrant-project.eu/ontology/dependability-index#> .
@prefix mit:     <https://warrant-project.eu/ontology/mitigation#> .
@prefix dt:      <https://warrant-project.eu/ontology/digital-twin#> .
@prefix scen:    <https://warrant-project.eu/ontology/scenario#> .
@prefix owl:     <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix rdf:     <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#> .
@prefix rdfs:    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix xsd:     <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .


# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# VESSEL
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

ll1:ContainerVessel_LL1 a davom:Vessel ;
    warrant:hasIdentifier "LL1-VESSEL-V145" ;
    warrant:hasName "LL1 Containership (Danaos — CATHERINE C, V145)" ;
    warrant:hasDescription "Danaos containership hull 4009/4010/4011/4012. Reference vessel for WARRaNT LL1. Navigation and communications OT scope." .


# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# VESSEL FUNCTIONS
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

ll1:NavigationFunction a davom:VesselFunction ;
    warrant:hasIdentifier "VF-NAV-001" ;
    warrant:hasName "Navigation Function" ;
    warrant:hasDescription "Position fixing, chart display (ECDIS), course keeping (Autopilot), target tracking (RADAR), and speed/depth sensing." .

ll1:CommunicationFunction a davom:VesselFunction ;
    warrant:hasIdentifier "VF-COM-001" ;
    warrant:hasName "Communication Function" ;
    warrant:hasDescription "Short-range (VHF), long-range (MF/HF), satellite (Inmarsat-C/GX/FBB), and AIS vessel identification and distress alerting." .

ll1:VoyageRecordingFunction a davom:VesselFunction ;
    warrant:hasIdentifier "VF-VDR-001" ;
    warrant:hasName "Voyage Data Recording Function" ;
    warrant:hasDescription "Black-box voyage data recording (VDR/SVDR) for accident investigation; receives audio, video, serial, and contact inputs from bridge equipment." .

ll1:ElectricalAlarmFunction a davom:VesselFunction ;
    warrant:hasIdentifier "VF-EL-001" ;
    warrant:hasName "Electrical OT and Alarm Monitoring Function" ;
    warrant:hasDescription "Alarm monitoring system (AMS), bridge navigational watch alarm (BNWAS), fire detection, general alarm, and navigation lights." .

ll1:ContainerVessel_LL1 davom:hasFunction ll1:NavigationFunction .
ll1:ContainerVessel_LL1 davom:hasFunction ll1:CommunicationFunction .
ll1:ContainerVessel_LL1 davom:hasFunction ll1:VoyageRecordingFunction .
ll1:ContainerVessel_LL1 davom:hasFunction ll1:ElectricalAlarmFunction .


# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# SYSTEMS
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

ll1:NavigationSystem a davom:TechnicalSystem ;
    warrant:hasIdentifier "SYS-NAV-001" ;
    warrant:hasName "Bridge Navigation System" ;
    warrant:hasDescription "Integrated bridge navigation system covering RADAR, ECDIS, GPS, Gyrocompass, Autopilot, Speedlog, Echosounder, Anemometer, AIS, and BNWAS." .

ll1:CommunicationSystem a davom:CommunicationSystem ;
    warrant:hasIdentifier "SYS-COM-001" ;
    warrant:hasName "Bridge Communication System" ;
    warrant:hasDescription "Ship communication system: VHF (×3), MF/HF, Inmarsat-C (×2), Inmarsat GX, Inmarsat FBB-500, Navtex, and Communications PC." .

ll1:VDRSystem a davom:TechnicalSystem ;
    warrant:hasIdentifier "SYS-VDR-001" ;
    warrant:hasName "Voyage Data Recorder System (SVDR)" ;
    warrant:hasDescription "Simplified Voyage Data Recorder: FURUNO VR-7017, sw 24550103-01.63. Central data hub receiving serial, contact, video, and audio from all bridge OT." .

ll1:AlarmMonitoringSystem a davom:CyberPhysicalSystem ;
    warrant:hasIdentifier "SYS-AMS-001" ;
    warrant:hasName "Alarm Monitoring System (AMS)" ;
    warrant:hasDescription "Integrated alarm monitoring system for engine room and vessel-wide alerting. Interconnected with VDR and BNWAS via SERIAL/CONTACT. FMEA RPN=315 (highest in electrical OT)." .

ll1:NavigationFunction      davom:isSupportedBy ll1:NavigationSystem .
ll1:CommunicationFunction   davom:isSupportedBy ll1:CommunicationSystem .
ll1:VoyageRecordingFunction davom:isSupportedBy ll1:VDRSystem .
ll1:VoyageRecordingFunction davom:isSupportedBy ll1:NavigationSystem .
ll1:ElectricalAlarmFunction davom:isSupportedBy ll1:AlarmMonitoringSystem .


